        "11": {
          "id": "11",
          "name": "time-dilation.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n\n    <!-- link back to the main gallery page -->\n    <a class=\"back-link\" href=\"index.html\">Back to Becoming Super</a>\n\n    <!-- Title -->\n    <h1>Racing the Clock: Time Dilation and The Flash</h1>\n\n    <!-- hero image -->\n    <div class=\"page-image\">\n      <img src=\"light-trails.jpg\" alt=\"Long-exposure photo of glowing light trails from fast-moving traffic at night\">\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- informational paragraphs -->\n    <p>The Flash's super speed connects directly to one of the strangest ideas in physics: time dilation. According to Einstein's theory of relativity, time doesn't pass at the same rate for everyone. Instead, how fast time moves depends on how fast you're moving relative to someone else.</p>\n\n    <p>Specifically, as an object's speed gets closer to the speed of light, time slows down for that object relative to an observer who is standing still. This isn't just a theory: it's been measured directly. Extremely precise atomic clocks flown on airplanes tick at very slightly different rates than identical clocks left on the ground, exactly as relativity predicts.</p>\n\n    <p>If The Flash could truly approach light speed, time would pass dramatically slower for him than for everyone else. From his perspective, he might only experience a few seconds while hours pass in the outside world, which is part of what lets him seem to \"freeze time\" in the comics.</p>\n\n    <p>The long-exposure photo above hints at this idea in a more everyday way: because the camera's shutter stays open for several seconds, fast-moving headlights and taillights get stretched into continuous streaks of light instead of single points, visually connecting speed and the passage of time.</p>\n\n    <!-- image citation -->\n    <div class=\"citation\">\n      <p>Photo by <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/@robinpierre?utm_source=unsplash&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_content=creditCopyText\">Robin Pierre</a> on <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/high-angle-photography-of-road-at-nighttime-dPgPoiUIiXk?utm_source=unsplash&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_content=creditCopyText\">Unsplash</a></p>\n    </div>\n\n  </body>\n</html>\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "12": {
          "id": "12",
          "name": "lift-and-aerodynamics.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n\n    <!-- link back to the main gallery page -->\n    <a class=\"back-link\" href=\"index.html\">Back to Becoming Super</a>\n\n    <!-- Title -->\n    <h1>The Science of Lift: How Superman Could \"Fly\"</h1>\n\n    <!-- hero image -->\n    <div class=\"page-image\">\n      <img src=\"airplane-wing.jpg\" alt=\"An airplane wing cutting through clouds during flight\">\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- informational paragraphs -->\n    <p>Superman's flight is one of the most iconic superhero abilities, but the physics behind real flight comes down to a concept called lift. Lift is the upward force that allows airplanes, birds, and even flying superheroes to overcome gravity.</p>\n\n    <p>One explanation for lift involves Bernoulli's principle, which states that faster-moving air has lower pressure than slower-moving air. On an airplane wing, air travels over the curved top surface and under the flatter bottom surface. If the air moves faster over the top, the pressure there drops relative to the pressure underneath, creating a net upward force.</p>\n\n    <p>In reality, lift is a bit more complicated than Bernoulli's principle alone. Engineers also point to Newton's third law: as a wing pushes air downward, the air pushes the wing upward in return. Both explanations work together to describe how thousands of pounds of metal can stay airborne.</p>\n\n    <p>So while Superman doesn't have wings, the same core idea applies to any flying object: redirect enough air downward, and something will push back up. Whether it's a jet wing or a superhero's cape, the underlying physics is what keeps things aloft.</p>\n\n    <!-- image citation -->\n    <div class=\"citation\">\n      <p>Photo by <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/@rparmly?utm_source=unsplash&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_content=creditCopyText\">Ross Parmly</a> on <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/aerial-photography-of-airliner-rf6ywHVkrlY?utm_source=unsplash&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_content=creditCopyText\">Unsplash</a></p>\n    </div>\n\n  </body>\n</html>\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        },
        "13": {
          "id": "13",
          "name": "refraction-and-diffraction.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n  </head>\n  <body>\n\n    <!-- link back to the main gallery page -->\n    <a class=\"back-link\" href=\"index.html\">Back to Becoming Super</a>\n\n    <!-- Title -->\n    <h1>Bending Light: The Optics Behind Invisibility</h1>\n\n    <!-- hero image -->\n    <div class=\"page-image\">\n      <img src=\"light-refraction.jpg\" alt=\"A glass prism splitting white light into a rainbow of colors\">\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- informational paragraphs -->\n    <p>The Invisible Woman's power hinges on controlling light itself. To understand how that might work, it helps to understand two related concepts in optics: refraction and diffraction.</p>\n\n    <p>Refraction happens when light passes from one material into another and changes speed, causing it to bend. This is exactly what happens inside a glass prism: as white light enters the glass, each wavelength (color) bends by a slightly different amount, spreading the light out into a visible rainbow.</p>\n\n    <p>Diffraction is a related but distinct phenomenon: it's the bending and spreading of light waves as they pass around an obstacle or through a small opening. Instead of light traveling in a straight line, diffraction causes it to spread out, similar to ripples bending around a rock in a pond.</p>\n\n    <p>If someone could precisely control how light refracts and diffracts around their body, light rays could theoretically be redirected around them entirely, letting light from behind pass through to an observer's eyes as if nothing were there. In practice, this kind of precise, whole-body control over light doesn't currently exist, but researchers have used related principles to develop early \"metamaterials\" that can bend light in unusual ways.</p>\n\n    <!-- image citation -->\n    <div class=\"citation\">\n      <p>Photo by <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/@davidclode?utm_source=unsplash&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_content=creditCopyText\">David Clode</a> on <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-colorful-object-with-a-rainbow-light-coming-out-of-it-0ftkrjhuabI?utm_source=unsplash&amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;utm_content=creditCopyText\">Unsplash</a></p>\n    </div>\n\n  </body>\n</html>\n",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        }
      },
